## 🎯 Objectives

1. Understand Binary-Coded Decimal representation.
2. Understand BCD addition.
3. Understand why BCD correction is required.
4. Design a BCD Adder using Verilog HDL.
5. Write a Verilog testbench.
6. Verify the design using simulation.
7. Upload and document the project on GitHub.

## ⭐ Advantages

* Directly represents decimal digits.
* Useful in decimal arithmetic systems.
* Easy to display decimal results using digital displays.
* Suitable for educational understanding of BCD arithmetic.

## ⚠️ Disadvantages

* Requires more bits than pure binary representation.
* Additional correction logic is required.
* Hardware complexity is higher than ordinary binary addition.

## 📚 Applications

BCD adders are used in:

* Digital calculators
* Digital clocks
* Electronic counters
* Seven-segment display systems
* Decimal arithmetic circuits
* Financial and commercial computing systems

## 🔄 BCD Adder vs Binary Adder

| Feature               | Binary Adder        | BCD Adder                |
| --------------------- | ------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Number representation | Binary              | Decimal digits in BCD    |
| Correction            | Not required        | Required when result > 9 |
| Hardware              | Simpler             | More complex             |
| Decimal display       | Requires conversion | Directly suitable        |
| Application           | General arithmetic  | Decimal arithmetic       |

## ✅ Result

The **1-digit BCD Adder** was successfully designed and simulated using Verilog HDL.

The testbench verified BCD additions both with and without correction. The simulation results match the expected BCD arithmetic results.

